# Issue report: Gate output fixture

Issue `<ISSUE>` · symptom `white-screen` · environment `local`

## Verdict

**Probable cause** (confidence: medium-high). Symptom reproduced: `could-not-test`.

Most likely cause: Duplicate declaration: plugin-a + plugin-b (CONFIRMED). function my_helper is declared in plugin-a and plugin-b. When both load, PHP stops with "Cannot redeclare" and the page is blank (fatal-if-both-load).

## Hypotheses

### Duplicate declaration: plugin-a + plugin-b — CONFIRMED (score 11.5)

function my_helper is declared in plugin-a and plugin-b. When both load, PHP stops with "Cannot redeclare" and the page is blank (fatal-if-both-load).

- Supported by: log `PHP Fatal error: Cannot redeclare my_helper() (previously declared in <ROOT>/wp-content/plugins/plugin-a/plugin-a.php:5) in <ROOT>/wp-content/plugins/plugin-b/plugin-b.php on lin`, observation `wp-content/plugins/plugin-a/plugin-a.php:8`
- Independently re-checked sources: log, observation
- PASSED evidence:wp-content/plugins/plugin-a/plugin-a.php:8: construct found at line 8
- PASSED evidence:wp-content/plugins/plugin-b/plugin-b.php:8: construct found at line 8
- PASSED support:log: the log clue is still in evidence.json
- PASSED php-l:wp-content/plugins/plugin-a/plugin-a.php: no syntax error
- PASSED php-l:wp-content/plugins/plugin-b/plugin-b.php: no syntax error
- PASSED declaration-sites: all 2 cited declarations were re-read and each still declares my_helper
- note: both declaration sites were re-read and verified: they count as two deterministic supports

## Resolution options

### For: Duplicate declaration: plugin-a + plugin-b

- **[needs-a-human] human: wp-content/plugins/plugin-a/plugin-a.php:8** — no automatic patch: not custom code (unknown: not inside any customPaths entry: ownership is not assumed (a plugin name or location does not prove it is custom). Add it to customPaths to allow fix tasks.)
- **[send-to-claude-code] patch: Wrap function my_helper() in a function_exists() guard** — my_helper is declared here and also elsewhere; if both files load, PHP stops with "Cannot redeclare". The guard makes the first one that loads win. It does NOT decide which copy should win: that is a decision for the owner (see the other resolution options). Note that a guarded function is defined when the line runs, not at compile time.
    - PASSED diff-applies: the diff applies cleanly to the original text and gives exactly the new text
    - PASSED php-l: the patched file has no syntax error
    - PASSED scanner-rerun: the PHP scanner no longer reports the problem in the patched text
    - NOT verified: The oracle has not been re-run with this patch applied; that happens in 'issue retest' (against a fixed copy or the sandbox).

```diff
--- a/wp-content/plugins/plugin-b/plugin-b.php
+++ b/wp-content/plugins/plugin-b/plugin-b.php
@@ -5,8 +5,10 @@
  */
 defined( 'ABSPATH' ) || exit;
 
-function my_helper( $x ) {
-	return $x . 'b';
+if ( ! function_exists( 'my_helper' ) ) {
+	function my_helper( $x ) {
+		return $x . 'b';
+	}
 }
 add_filter( 'the_title', 'my_helper' );
```

- **[needs-a-human] plugin: Deactivate, replace or update Plugin A** — Switching off Plugin A 1.0.0 would stop: whatever it provides (no feature in the table maps to it; read its description: none). Of the two plugins that declare the same name, deactivate the one whose features you need least, or ask its author to prefix the name.
    - NOT verified: Which plugin to deactivate is a decision for the site owner; Plugin Guard does not make it.
- **[needs-a-human] plugin: Deactivate, replace or update Plugin B** — Switching off Plugin B 1.0.0 would stop: whatever it provides (no feature in the table maps to it; read its description: none). Of the two plugins that declare the same name, deactivate the one whose features you need least, or ask its author to prefix the name.
    - NOT verified: Which plugin to deactivate is a decision for the site owner; Plugin Guard does not make it.

